{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Multi-level marketing today is a graveyard. \nNearly every MLM giant has been crushed.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5040,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tupperware—which built its empire on mid-century \nhousewives seeking recognition, autonomy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10160,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and purpose—went bankrupt in 20 24. Avon, \nwhich promised women for generations that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they could turn their social circles into cash \nselling cosmetics, got dissolved that same year.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20720,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "NewAge, which sold millennials the dream of making \nmoney from anywhere by dropshipping superfood  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25360,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supplements and CBD beverages straight from their \nphones—collapsed into bankruptcy just two years  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30720,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before Avon and Tupperware. Medifast, which sells \nweight-loss coaching and meal replacements to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36160,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vulnerable, desperate dieters, has seen its \nvaluation evaporate 96% from nearly $300 to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42800,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$11 a share. Nu Skin, which peddles anti-aging \ndevices and skincare tech, has fallen from $90  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49120,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to single digits. And the biggest MLM of \nall, Herbalife, is down over 80%. In short,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "anyone who's invested in an MLM in the past \ndecade has lost just about all of their money. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60640,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like today's prediction markets and online \ncasinos, MLMs walk a thin line between legal and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "illegal, and they intentionally ride the gray \narea as long as possible. Despite operating in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69920,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "completely separate categories, all of these \ncompanies do the same things. They promise  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "life-changing fortunes, empowerment, freedom, \nand entrepreneurship to anyone willing to sell.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80000,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They all claim their products are too advanced and \ninnovative to ever sit on store shelves, so the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only way to buy these groundbreaking inventions \nis behind closed doors through individual reps.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89360,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They parade scientists, doctors, nutritionists, \nor PhDs in white lab coats to serve as proof that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95840,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their products actually work. They throw glamorous \nevents with dancers and motivational speakers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102720,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "give away cars, cruises, jewelry, and make sure \ntheir success and generosity is filmed for all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to see. And the best sellers are crowned crazy \ntitles like Presidential Black Diamond Executive,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114080,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "talk up their lifestyles as new millionaires, \nand hammer home that if you work just as hard  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119760,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as they did selling these products \n- with enough perseverance, you too  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121840,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can one day be on the stage just like them.\nAnd wherever these companies go, controversy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is sure to follow. Tupperware cooked its books \nfor years by dumping inventory onto sellers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who had never ordered it and recording it all \nas revenue. Avon bribed government officials  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with millions in cash and luxury bags to secure a \nbusiness license ahead of other MLMs. Meanwhile,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141040,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mannatech trained sellers to target cancer \npatients and parents of children with Down  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145280,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "syndrome, framing its products as cures with \nfake clinical data. Nu Skin was fined for running  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150720,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "illegal, high-pressure recruitment rings \nand hiding its pyramid from regulators.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155280,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Herbalife hustled low-income minorities into \na selling frenzy with get-rich-quick spiels  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for decades until the FTC fined it $200M.\nYet none of these scandals ever seemed to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "materially hurt their businesses. Whenever these \ncompanies crossed the line in one country, they'd  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simply pack up and redeploy the same playbook in \nanother country with weaker regulation and fewer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175840,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consumer protections. Keep in mind—the companies \nwe've named are just the few that got big enough  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181040,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to IPO. Beyond the public markets lies an even \nbigger graveyard filled with generations of dead,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186880,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bankrupt, and defunct MLMs that peddled \neverything from essential oils and leggings  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191680,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to miracle juices and survival seeds.\nIn a healthy business, you make money by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196080,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "selling to customers. In an MLM, you make money by \nrecruiting people who become your customers. When  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201840,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the seller and the customer are the same person, \n\"revenue\" can be manufactured. This is why MLMs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are regularly accused of being pyramid schemes. In \na pyramid scheme, you make money from recruitment:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212720,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you pay to join, you get paid when you recruit \nothers who also pay to join, and the money flows  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217760,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "upward from new entrants to the people above \nthem. Pyramid schemes are illegal because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they're guaranteed to collapse. Every level needs \na larger one beneath it to keep going. As soon as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226960,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "recruitment slows, the whole thing falls apart and \nthe majority stuck at the bottom are the biggest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231440,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "losers. An MLM is near-identical, except it sticks \na product into the equation. That product is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237520,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the entire legal defense. If the product is \nsold to real customers outside the pyramid,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242320,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then the business is deemed legal. But if sales \ncome from sellers who are buying products only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246640,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to qualify for commissions, then recruiting others \nto do the same, then it becomes a pyramid scheme. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the line every MLM walks: they survive \nby keeping just enough product moving to real  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "customers to stay legal, while the real money and \ngrowth come from recruitment. A normal business  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262720,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pays out of its own pocket to acquire customers \nthrough marketing, advertising, real estate, a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268160,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "salaried sales force, or all of the above. An MLM \nexternalizes all of these costs onto a voluntary,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275360,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unpaid army that works purely on commission. \nThe company stays asset-light—which is why, even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "though MLMs' operating margins look weak, their \nreturn on invested capital can be extraordinary. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285840,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's easy to cry \"scam\" and write off every MLM \nas a pyramid scheme. But it's far more useful to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291680,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "understand why the model exists at all, what \nproblem it solves, and the organizational and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296320,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "psychological strategies the biggest players \nuse to exploit millions of people every year.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301440,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The model is scummy but it's also extraordinarily \neffective. If dangling money and a rags-to-riches  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306720,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dream were all it took, every consumer \nbrand on earth would run some form of MLM,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "instead of collectively burning billions every \nquarter on Facebook and Instagram ads for a measly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1% conversion. A massive global sales force that \ncosts nothing in real estate or payroll, works  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322320,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "purely on commission, and sells passionately and \npersistently is a powerful thing for any company  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327760,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to have. In this episode, we analyze the economics \nof MLMs, from Tupperware to Herbalife to Nu Skin,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334800,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "break down the six telltale signs that define \nevery MLM, and explain why—for the company,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340240,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and for everyone in the pyramid \nbeneath it—the good times never last. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344480,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "MLMs started in North America in the 1940s as \na twist on traditional direct sales. A salesman  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350640,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "knocked on doors, sold a brush or appliance, and \nearned a commission on whatever he moved that day.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These were the original gig workers who got paid \npurely on performance with no salary. This model  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dominated for generations until a vitamin company \ncalled Nutrilife changed things up. Instead of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366640,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "earning only off their own sales, salesmen could \nnow make money by recruiting other salesmen  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371360,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and taking a cut of whatever their recruits \nsold. That was the innovation. At their core,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376320,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "MLMs don't change how people sell—only how they're \npaid. Someone still has to pound the pavement,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381440,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "demo the product, and close the sale.\nTupperware became the poster child for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385760,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "MLMs. The company grabbed this new business \nmodel and rode it to global success,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390400,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doubling sales through the '60s and '70s. Before \nMLM, Tupperware's plastic food containers had been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rotting on shelves—customers couldn't grasp the \nvalue of the product through print ads. Yet once  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401360,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the company switched to direct sales, business \nexploded. It targeted suburban housewives to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406400,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be both customers and sellers, teaching them to \nsell from their living rooms by inviting friends,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411200,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "family, and neighbors over for casual \nget-togethers. After all the cake and gossip,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they'd launch into pre-planned product demos, \nsales routines, and recruitment pitches. Guests  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420960,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be convinced of Tupperware's merit \nor simply pressured to buy out of guilt  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or courtesy. The host held every advantage—she \ncontrolled the venue, hand-picked the guests,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429600,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and pocketed 25–35% of every container sold. And \nif she recruited her buyers into becoming sellers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "themselves, she earned a cut of their sales too.\nUltimately, the pivot to MLM worked so well that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441440,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tupperware eventually pulled its products from \nstores entirely and made them available only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445840,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through its growing pyramid of housewives. But \nMLM was just an accelerant and not the root cause  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Tupperware's success. In the mid-century, \nthe company had no real competition or retail  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456320,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equivalent. With a patented airtight seal and \nproprietary plastic, there was genuine merit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461520,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to its products. Tupperware's real legacy was the \nplaybook it handed to modern MLMs—who swapped out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467440,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its one redeeming feature for overpriced \ngeneric goods and predatory recruitment,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471840,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where sellers are pushed to build pyramids as wide \nand deep as possible rather than actually sell  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "product. Once Tupperware's patents expired in the \n'80s and '90s, the company became a prisoner to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482480,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its own sales channel. Once the patents expired, \ncheap alternatives flooded store shelves—Ziploc,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488320,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gladware, Rubbermaid—and Tupperware lost \nits moat. There was no longer any reason  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492880,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to sit through a living-room party just to \nbuy plastic containers, and the housewives  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496880,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who hosted them were now working 9-to-5 jobs.\nAs U.S. sales slid through the '90s and 2000s,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502800,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tupperware tried to crawl back to retail, \nsigning a deal with Target. But its sellers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507120,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "felt betrayed—why pour unpaid hours into chasing \ncommission when the company was now selling the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512000,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same products on a shelf and poaching their \ncustomers? Sellers quit, revenue fell further,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516960,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and retail threatened to destroy the only channel \nkeeping the company alive. Tupperware hastily  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522160,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "abandoned the Target deal and retreated to direct \nsales. The channel had become the product—and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from here on, the company had no choice but \nto lean deeper into MLM just to survive. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thus, the first sign of an MLM is a company that \ncontinually adapts its products and brand to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537600,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "feed its sellers. This is the exact opposite of a \ntraditional brand, which pushes a single identity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "value prop, and core positioning across every \nmarket so that it means the same thing everywhere  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it operates. In contrast, an MLM reshapes itself \ninto whatever it needs to be to make the next  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sale. Through the 2000s, Tupperware slapped its \nlogo on whatever was trending. Because its sellers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560320,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worked purely on commission, the company couldn't \nmark down its products to move inventory without  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enraging the very people it depended on—so it just \nkept adding more products to sell. In the U.S.,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570400,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it sold baby bottles, educational toys, pressure \ncookers, and knives. In China, water filters;  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576880,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in Korea, kimchi holders; in India, branded \nwater bottles; in Europe, food processors,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583440,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "specialty sauce makers, cookbooks, and \nmicrosteamers. There was nothing inherently wrong  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588080,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with any of the products. But beyond the plastic \ncontainers, they were all generic commodities  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592720,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "picked out of a third-party catalog and dressed \nup in Tupperware's colors and logo. The company  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597680,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would rebrand a product just enough to make it \nlook like a special, groundbreaking exclusive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601920,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "invention that its sellers could pitch—even \nthrough the same item sat on a store shelf  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and sold for much less under a different name.\nSo while MLMs like Tupperware always portray  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611520,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "themselves as innovators, the business is \nreally no different from a dropshipper's:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you source it, brand it, mark it up, and \nmove it. As a result, MLMs have relentless  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "product pipelines—sellers need something fresh, \nexciting, and trendy to pitch every quarter,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and they can't wait three to five years for real \nR\u0026D. Everything comes down to spotting a trend,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631360,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slapping on the logo, marking it up, and shipping \nit out. Under this lens, products are just an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636160,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ever-widening net that's cast out every month to \npull as many people into the pyramid as possible. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641600,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The second sign is that an MLM product can \nnever be cheap, and must always have some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645840,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theatrical element. It has to be priced at \na premium, so the average seller feels the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650160,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "commission is worth the time, money, and \nsocial capital they'll invest in making a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654240,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sale—but never so expensive it's out of reach \nof the mass market, because the business still  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658480,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "runs on volume. It must also involve some kind of \ndemo, or the direct-sales channel has no reason  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to exist. And it must be framed as a lifestyle \ninvestment—something financial or personal to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "justify the premium. So by default, every MLM \nproduct is overpriced and lives in a paradox  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674560,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where it's too complex to be sold in stores, \nyet needs to be simple enough to wow a room. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679760,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The third sign is that recruiting is the lifeblood \nof every MLM. It didn't start that way for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tupperware, but it quickly became the backbone \nonce the company lost its product moat. An MLM  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690080,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will only ever go as far as the size of its sales \nforce—the more sellers, the more pitches they'll  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694640,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make, and the more shots on goal, the better the \nsales will be. Direct sales is a brutal field:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699840,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with no salary to fall back on, attrition is \nabsurdly high. It's a sales force that can never  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705280,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grow on its own and the channel is more like a \nleaking bucket that companies must constantly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709920,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "refill just to stay where they are. There's a \nhard cap on how much any one person can sell,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714800,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "since there are only so many hours in a day, \nso many prospects in an area, and so much  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719520,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "utility a product can offer. Thus, when an MLM has \nno product moat, the business inevitably devolves  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725040,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into recruiting. But the question is: what gets \nthese people to actively sell, recruit, and stay? "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730480,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An MLM has no sticks. Sellers aren't employees. \nThere are no salaries to dock, no managers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736880,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no performance reviews, and no way to fire \nsomeone or order them to work harder. In short,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the company has little control or visibility into \nits sales force. The only thing it can control  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746960,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the structure those sellers operate in, and \nthe only tool it has are carrots—cash bonuses,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752480,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fancy prizes, luxury trips, recognition, and \ntitles, all of which Tupperware learned to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758000,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dangle in front of its sellers. Every pyramid \nis built on two essentials: a promotion track  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that gets you to the top and a quota system at \nthe base. The promotion track is a career matrix  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768320,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of titles and escalating rewards that pushes \nbuyers to become sellers and sellers to become  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773760,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "recruiters. The quota system is the minimum volume \nevery seller has to move to stay in that matrix.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779600,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This kind of gamification isn't unique to MLMs as \ncareer matrixes exist everywhere in the corporate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785280,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "world. MLMs simply took that psychology \nand stripped out the salary, job security,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and most of the substance. Under this lens, an \nMLM operates a lot more like an HR department  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795680,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where it's powerless on its own, does little of \nthe actual work, but designs the structure and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800240,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pay bands to reward the behavior it wants.\nTupperware learned the hard way that every  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804800,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "market needs its own incentives—and like the \nproducts, the rewards have to feel fresh,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809360,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exciting, and worth chasing quarter \nafter quarter. For MLMs, there are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814000,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only three levers to the business. The first is \nrecruitment—bringing in new sellers and growing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819120,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the sales force. The second is activation—getting \nexisting sellers off their couches and into the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field. The third is productivity—getting active \nsellers to move even more volume. Of the three,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830320,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "recruitment is the only one with no ceiling. \nYou can only activate the sellers you have,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834800,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and you can only push a seller so far before \nthey hit the limit of their hours, their network,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839920,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the product itself. Recruitment is the \nonly lever that scales and boosts the other two  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844800,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "—which is why every MLM is structurally forced to \noptimize for \"more sellers\" - rather than \"better  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sellers.\" The model pushes every seller to build \ntheir own pyramid, and the company sits on top as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855520,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the single pyramid that contains all the others.\nBut activation is equally important. When  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discount buyers flood the base of the pyramid, \nproductivity craters and the real sellers get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865040,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dragged down. Tupperware learned to be \nruthless about weeding out the bargain  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hunters and inactive sellers—and this in turn \ncreates another paradox. If the product is good  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874320,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough that people are happy to buy it at \na discount without ever becoming sellers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878400,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then no one has a reason to join the pyramid. A \ncustomer who only buys for themselves must stay  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "outside the pyramid otherwise they will drag down \nthe levels above them. Each layer stops feeding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888480,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the one above it, the structure stops sustaining \nitself, and the pyramid collapses inward. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tupperware held weekly local rallies where \nsellers would march, cheer, and sing together.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899200,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was an intoxicating and powerful draw for \nunderappreciated mothers and housewives who  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902960,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "received little validation from society or their \nspouses for raising children or running a home. At  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908880,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these events, these overlooked housewives would \nbecome the center of the room and be awarded  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 912960,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ribbons, pins, and applause for their efforts \nto sell Tupperware. This was activation in its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918480,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cheapest and most potent form as it cost the \ncompany almost nothing, converted recruits on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923360,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spot, reactivated idle sellers, and sent them all \nback into the field, energized to chase the next  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929280,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "round. Meanwhile the top sellers got to attend \na glamorous convention at HQ where they were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "awarded mink coats, diamonds, and keys to new \ncars. The company would also bury televisions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940080,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "washing machines, and jewelry in a field and then \nhand the best sellers a shovel to dig out their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944640,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prizes in front of a cheering crowd. All of this \nspectacle and success was filmed and publicized,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949120,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by design, to remind the rest of the sales force \nthat they too, could make it there with enough  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953760,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hustle and volume. In short, Tupperware just \nstaged the spectacle, materialized the carrots,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and let peer pressure and jealousy do the rest.\nThe fifth sign of an MLM is relentless expansion.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964400,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The only way to keep the pyramid intact is to \nkeep stacking more pyramids on top of it. When  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one market weakens or clamps down, the only path \nforward is to chase a new one, so there's always  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 974240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fresh growth to offset churn. As Americans and \nEuropeans slowly caught on that being a seller  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979440,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was nowhere near as easy as advertised—and \nthat the same products sat on store shelves  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983680,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for less—Tupperware's sales force and business in \nthe West began to slide. By the 20 10s, seven of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every ten dollars came from the developing world, \nwhere regulation and scrutiny of MLMs were far  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weaker and low employment made the pitch land even \nharder. Tupperware chased women in those countries  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and bent the brand to fit the local culture.\nIn Indonesia, it hijacked social lottery clubs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005680,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where women met to draw lots and got them to \nreframe Tupperware products as the jackpot. In  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011040,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "India, it called sellers \"lifestyle consultants\" \nand taught them to frame their sales events as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1015600,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prestigious seminars on financial literacy and \nentrepreneurship even though they were really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020480,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just selling water bottles and containers. In \nMexico and Brazil, where door-to-door selling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1025040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had been normalized for generations, it elevated \nthe everyday hustle into glamour and spectacle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "handing out jewelry, new cars, and luxury \nvacations at televised events to portray  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035520,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tupperware as an escape for women trapped \nin low incomes and castes. Ultimately,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040160,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when you run a pyramid of pyramids, every \npyramid matters, no matter how small. It's why  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1044880,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tupperware refused to pull out of Venezuela during \nhyperinflation or Egypt during the Arab Spring. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1050720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But by 2019, Tupperware had been disrupted on \nevery front. The smartphone enabled better-paying  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1056320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and simpler gig work with the advent of rideshare \nand food delivery. The sales force shrank year  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061360,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after year, and the cost structure turned lethal. \nCommission is a variable cost: when revenue falls,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067200,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "selling costs fall with it. But that does \nnothing for the fixed overhead of factories,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1071760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wholesale contracts, and the salaried corporate \nlayer that manages every country. Tupperware was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1077040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the worst of both worlds, operating as both an MLM \nand product company. It couldn't fully outsource  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1082720,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "manufacturing, because its lifetime-warranty and \nquality promise was the last thing separating  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its core products from generic Walmart knockoffs.\nThe company briefly recovered during COVID as the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1093200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "world was thrown back into the low-information, \nunder-retailed, isolated conditions where MLM  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1098240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "businesses historically thrive in. Yet as society \nreopened, business once again dropped like a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1103440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stone. Every underlying problem returned. The \npost-COVID generation valued time and freedom,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1108800,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the old carrots of jewelry, trips, and career \nladders no longer tempted them. At the same time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1114320,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tupperware had become a victim of market \nevolution. The category that the company  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1118240,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had pioneered had been upended as consumers \nmoved away from plastic and towards glass and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1122800,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stainless steel. Combined with a declining \nsales force and outdated sales channel,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1127120,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every pyramid crumbled one by one, and \nthe company they held up went bankrupt. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1131760,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Modern MLMs studied Tupperware's flaws and \nengineered around them. No company did it more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1137120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "notoriously and successfully than Herbalife. \nRegistered in the Cayman Islands, Herbalife  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1142320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "started in the U.S. in the 1980s selling powdered \nprotein shakes for weight loss. The product was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1147920,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nothing special, which is exactly why its founders \nwent to MLM in the first place—leaning on private  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1152800,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in-person sales and invite-only recruitment \nseminars to push a product that lacked the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1157920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "science and brand power to earn a place on store \nshelves. Sellers waved before-and-after photos  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1163200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in high-pressure pitches and positioned the \nshakes as a dietary breakthrough. Naturally,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1168000,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its biggest market was Americans.\nBy the 2000s, Herbalife faced  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1172000,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tupperware's exact pressures—consumers \nwere getting online, researching nutrition,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1176400,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and becoming better educated as supplement stores \nspread nationwide, stocking competing products. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1182800,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We talk a lot on Modern MBA about market leaders \nand the moats that protect them. Right now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1187840,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s said that every day your business is late \nto AI, you’re falling two days behind.​ But how  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1192240,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do you keep up? The competition is only moving \nfaster.​ Fortunately, there’s NetSuite Next.​"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1197600,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You already know NetSuite - it’s the gold \nstandard AI-powered business management  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1201520,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "suite that brings your financials, inventory, \ncommerce, HR, and CRM into one single source  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1208080,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of truth. NetSuite is trusted by over 43,000 \ncustomers and securely connects all your data."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1214320,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "NetSuite Next is the next generation and an \nevolution in how your business gets done,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1219280,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because AI is built into everything you \ndo. NetSuite Next automatically surfaces  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1224320,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "custom insights throughout your day, deploys AI \nagents to work alongside you to solve problems  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1229440,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and handle your routine work. Anytime you \nhave a question about anything, just ask,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1233680,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it’ll answer, just like you’re having a \nconversation with a colleague. Like NetSuite,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1237680,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "NetSuite Next is built from the ground up to be \ncustomizable for a wide range of industries, so it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1242960,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supports the way your business works rather than \nthe other way around. Whether your company earns  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1247600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "millions or even hundreds of millions, it’s time \nfor NetSuite Next, where your business meets AI."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1252960,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At Modern MBA, we take great pride in our accuracy \nand originality. We don’t use AI to write scripts,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1259440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do research, analyze data and we certainly don’t \ntrust them to handle our accounting, finances,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1264880,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and payroll. But NetSuite Next is completely \ndifferent - it’s AI built from the ground up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1269520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by businesses, for businesses that are serious \nabout getting to the next level. Once Modern  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1274480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "MBA scales to that seven figure milestone, \nwe plan to adopt NetSuite Next ourselves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1279760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the first time ever, you can try NetSuite \nNext for free. If your revenues are at least  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1284240,
    "dDurationMs": 8480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the seven figures, go to NetSuite \ndot AI slash MODERNMBA. Built for every  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1292720,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "industry. Ready for every boardroom. \nNetSuite dot AI slash MODERNMBA. Thank  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1295200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you to NetSuite for supporting Modern \nMBA and making this episode possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1300960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Herbalife had also been charged through the \n'90s by the FDA, Canada, and California over  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1306240,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "false medical claims where the company had \nbeen found guilty of teaching sellers to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1310320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "close deals by promising customers that they were \nguaranteed to lose weight with their purchase. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1315360,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But where Tupperware clung to its \npast, Herbalife reinvented itself  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1318880,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the modern age. It repositioned from \n\"weight loss\" to the broader, vaguer,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1323200,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "legally safe umbrella of \"nutrition and wellness,\" \nand changed how the product was sold. Instead of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1328720,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$120 tubs meant to last weeks, it distilled the \npowder into single servings—a tea, an aloe shot,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1335360,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and a shake—sold as a combo for 8 to $10. This \nshift to high-frequency low-commitment consumption  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1341760,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dramatically lowered the bar for a sale and forced \nbuyers into regular correspondence with their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1346800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sellers. Every interaction was another chance \nfor their seller to nudge them toward another  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1351680,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "purchase or toward becoming a seller themselves.\nBeneath it all, Herbalife ran an aggressive points  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1357040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system. Every product carried a fixed value, and \nthe only way to unlock higher commissions and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1362080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cheaper wholesale prices was to hit a monthly \nquota. This incentivized \"buying your way up,\"  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1367440,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as sellers could spend thousands on inventory \nand stash it in their garage just to move up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1372160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the ladder. Unlike Tupperware or a traditional \nbusiness, where you earn commission only when a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1377120,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "product reaches an end user, Herbalife sellers \nearned commission the moment their recruits  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1381600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ordered inventory from HQ. A recruit could trash \nthe product or pile it in their garage, and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1387040,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seller above them still profited—which pushed \neveryone climbing the ranks to keep pressuring the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1391920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rows below them to order more, regardless of \nwhether or not the product could ever be sold. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1396960,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yet Herbalife's real growth engine \nwas its clubs. In the early 2000s,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1401360,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "low-income communities in Mexico loved the \nproduct but couldn't afford a whole tub alone.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1405920,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Local sellers turned their living rooms into \nmakeshift cafés, gathered the neighborhood,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1410400,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and split a freshly mixed shake into single \nservings for $2-3. It was the Tupperware party  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1415760,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reborn, but with the new and critical addition \nof immediate, communal consumption. A Tupperware  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1420960,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "party sold you a product to take home; a \nHerbalife club had you drink it on the spot,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1425280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with everyone else. Drinking Herbalife became a \ndaily social ritual where people high-fived and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1430640,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "celebrated progress over drinks while the \nshared energy in the room fed the placebo.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1435360,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The only problem like before was scale: a \nliving room could only hold so many people. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1440160,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Herbalife systematized it, pushing sellers \nin other countries to pool their own money  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1444480,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for commercial spaces to dispense product, serve \ncustomers, host seminars, and store inventory.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1450800,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The company never put up a cent. It was the \nsellers who found the location, signed the lease,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1455600,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and split the rent, which only deepened their \ncommitment to the Herbalife pyramid. Instead of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1460240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one host pitching strangers in their living rooms, \nsellers rotated shifts to serve a constant stream  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1465200,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of attendees in a neutral, professional space. The \nvenue was an all-in-one social hub, sales floor,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1471440,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "recruitment center, and fulfillment warehouse. By \nthe early 20 10s, there were over 20,000 of these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1477120,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nutrition clubs in Mexico and thousands more \nin every major country, dispensing millions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1481920,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of shakes, teas, and shots to an army of regulars.\nAt this point, Herbalife faced the same crossroads  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1487680,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as Tupperware. The clubs were thriving—so why \nnot take the obvious next step, put up signage,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1492800,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "open the doors to the public, and turn them \ninto proper juice bars and neighborhood cafés?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1497520,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If formalized as retail storefronts, these \nvenues could capitalize on foot traffic and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1502080,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "move far more volume than they did as these \nunbranded, invite-only neighborhood clubs. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1506480,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yet Herbalife said no. Opening the doors to \nthe public would break the entire pyramid,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1510960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because every sale needed to flow through a \nspecific seller. To remain an MLM, Herbalife  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1515840,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clubs are strictly forbidden from becoming \nstores. There's never any exterior signage,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1520640,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "listings, advertising, and open windows. Entry is \nby invitation or referral only and no one walking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1526640,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by would even know the club was there unless \nthey were guided in. But there still needed to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1531040,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be some equal exchange of value so that sellers \nwould keep funding and operating these venues  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1535200,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out of pocket. So in return, Herbalife \ngave them legitimacy on the world stage. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1539760,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The company poured hundreds of millions into \nsports sponsorships to buy mainstream credibility,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1544320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "signing Cristiano Ronaldo as a brand ambassador, \nsponsoring FC Barcelona and a slew of other big  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1550000,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clubs, becoming the longest-running \njersey sponsor in MLS with LA Galaxy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1554480,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and partnering with national Olympic teams \naround the world. Herbalife would spend nearly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1558720,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a billion dollars on sponsorships over the \nnext 20 years. The logic was simple: no one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1563840,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would trust an unbranded storefront with blocked \nwindows, but they'd learn to recognize and trust  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1568560,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Herbalife name and brand if splashed across \nthe chests of the world's biggest athletes. That  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1573920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "association did all the advertising that the clubs \nwere legally forbidden from doing themselves.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1578960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Familiarity became a stand-in for legitimacy.\nInside, sellers were taught to give free \"wellness  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1583840,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evaluations\" as icebreakers to steer \nvulnerable customers into purchases  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1588000,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "under the guise of personalized nutrition. \nThere was no medical rigor as most of these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1592400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evaluations consisted of a simple weigh-in and \na handwritten questionnaire. To first-timers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1597520,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the clubs looked like cafés, but they were \nreally sales floors and recruitment centers. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1601760,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like Tupperware, Herbalife targeted \ncommunities that were communal, low-income,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1606400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "underemployed, yet motivated to move up. While \nTupperware rode the 1950s suburban housewife,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1611920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Herbalife rode the 21st-century Latino. For them, \nsoccer was a religion, and the sponsorships only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1617920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deepened their trust in the brand. In American \nmetros, Herbalife aggressively recruited Latinos  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1623280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who lacked the credentials, documentation, or \nEnglish proficiency to enter the white-collar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1628640,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "economy. Unlike corporate employers, Herbalife \ndidn't check immigration status and offered  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1633440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a rare pathway to non-manual work for people \nwho were stuck in low-end retail, restaurants,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1638480,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "construction sites, farms, and factories. \nBy the early 20 10s, the overwhelming  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1642880,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "majority of the U.S. pyramid was Latino.\nAcross Asia, Herbalife chased unemployed youth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1648400,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new grads, and part-time gym trainers—people \nwith the physical credibility to sell a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1653120,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wellness brand. While Tupperware leaned on \na loose calendar of rallies and conventions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1657600,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Herbalife took the same peer-pressure psychology \nto the next level to convert recruits and motivate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1661680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sellers. It built an escalating ladder of \nevents, each more exclusive than the last,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1666480,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and where access was gated by how much inventory \na seller was willing to buy. Those at the bottom  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1671840,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the pyramid started with free public pitches \nheld in hotel conference rooms and nutrition  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1676480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clubs. Attendees watched before-and-after photos \nand heard testimonials from customers who swore  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1681280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by the product, and top earners who swore by \nthe business. The goal was to get attendees to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1686160,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sign up as sellers on the spot. New recruits were \nthen funneled into the monthly training seminars  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1690720,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which were billed as essential product \ntraining, but in reality were meant to push  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1694560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "participants to buy more inventory on the spot.\nAbove that was the Leadership Development Weekend,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1699680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which was open only to the top performers who hit \nthe monthly quotas. These events were engineered  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1704480,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to bring sellers to their most emotionally \nvulnerable state through hours of shouting,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1709040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "singing, crying, and clapping. And at the very \ntop was the Extravaganza, where Herbalife packed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1715120,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an entire stadium with tens of thousands \nof sellers and brought on celebrities,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1719360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dancers, pyrotechnics, and tearful speeches \nfrom the top sliver of earners who had made  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1725040,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it. The best sellers were flown to luxury \nresorts and made to document every trip,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1729440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so the dream always looked close enough to touch \nfor the rows of sellers below them. The message at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1734480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every event was identical: anyone could get rich, \nso long as they committed fully, bought inventory,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1739760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and recruited relentlessly. While Tupperware's \nspectacle rewarded sellers for moving product,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1745280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Herbalife's rewarded sellers for buying it.\nWith brand awareness handled, Herbalife moved  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1750160,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to manufacture legitimacy for the \nproducts themselves. The company cut  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1754000,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back on outsourcing and began building its own \nfactories to claim ownership over quality and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1758880,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ingredients. It paid academics across the U.S. \nand Europe to produce cherry-picked clinical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1764000,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "studies asserting its products were superior \nto ordinary dieting. It bought the endorsement  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1768800,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a Nobel Prize-winning scientist for tens of \nmillions of dollars and slapped his name, face,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1773920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and credentials right on the label. These studies \nand scientists handed sellers an unassailable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1779200,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weapon against skeptics. By the mid-2010s, \nHerbalife was launching hundreds of products  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1784320,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a year tailed for every market like high-protein \niced coffee for Americans, rice-pudding flavors  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1789760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for Mexicans, dairy-free formulas for \nEuropeans, and kulfi shakes for Indians. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1794640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By 2015, the company had become the biggest MLM \non the American stock market. What Herbalife got  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1800160,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "right was selling consumables where Tupperware \nsold durables. Herbalife made money shipping  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1804960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "product to sellers; Tupperware only made money \non the containers carried home by real customers.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1810240,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This distinction enabled Herbalife to sustain \nearnings along with the appearance of recurring  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1814880,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "demand, because sellers were incentivized to \nkeep buying inventory regardless of whether  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1819600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or not anyone ever ingested the product. \nTupperware had no such cushion—its durables  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1824800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were one-time purchases built to last a lifetime. \nHerbalife's rapid growth, questionable accounting,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1830400,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and high pressure sales tactics caught \nthe attention of Pershing Square—one of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1834400,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wall Street's most aggressive hedge funds.\nBill Ackman was convinced that Herbalife  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1838800,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was a pyramid scheme—one where revenue came \nfrom sellers hoarding inventory rather than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1843200,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from genuine consumer demand. His conviction \nran so deep that he sank a billion dollars  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1847920,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into shorting the stock and spent tens of \nmillions more funding private investigators,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1852240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whistleblowers, and documentary filmmakers. \nHe lobbied politicians, civil-rights groups,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1857440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and regulators to investigate. As we covered in \nthe Chipotle video, burying companies in bad press  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1862960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was Ackman's MO—but with Herbalife he took it to \nan extreme, declaring on live television that he'd  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1868400,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ride his short \"to the end of the earth,\" and that \nthe only sane price for the company was zero. His  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1873680,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entire position rested on the belief that the \nU.S. government would agree with him, declare  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1877840,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Herbalife as an illegal pyramid scheme, and shut \ndown the company for good. No short seller in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1883040,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "history had ever been so publicly and financially \ncommitted to the destruction of a single company. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1887920,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What Ackman was asking for was not unreasonable. \nThe FTC had shut down companies in the past for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1893520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "being pyramid schemes like Equinox International \nin 2000, Trek Alliance in 2005. These were all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1899200,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "small, private, growing companies that operated \nat a fraction of the scale of Herbalife and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1903360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "didn't have the resources to survive the years \nof litigation. Yet the U.S. government had never  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1908240,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "successfully taken down a big, established MLM. \nThe last and only time in history it had tried  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1912640,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was 1979 and the FTC ended up losing. The courts \nfound Amway innocent of being a pyramid scheme  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1919120,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but guilty of price fixing and misrepresentation, \nwhich were lesser charges that the company easily  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1924080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "absorbed as slaps on the wrist. The FTC learned \nthat once a company scales to millions of sellers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1929920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grosses billions in sales, and can afford armies \nof lawyers, the bar for proving a pyramid scheme  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1935280,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "becomes impossibly high. Herbalife would become \nthe first major MLM in 30 years to face a federal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1941920,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "investigation. Thus, Ackman's ask was reasonable \n— but his bet on a successful conviction was crazy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1947600,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as it depended entirely on something that the \nU.S. government had never achieved in its history. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1952400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Herbalife went on an immediate counter-offensive. \nRather than wait for the government to decide,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1957040,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the company proactively rewired the very mechanics \nAckman was attacking. It rolled out generous buyer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1962640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "protections, offering first-time sellers a full \nrefund on starter kits and memberships within 90  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1968160,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "days along with a one-year money-back guarantee on \nall unsold inventory. With a return rate of just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1974240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1%, Herbalife argued it had disproved Ackman's \nassertion that poor people were losing their life  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1979680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "savings on inventory they couldn't sell—because if \nthat were true, they'd simply have sent it back. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1984720,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Ackman's biggest argument was that Herbalife's \nproducts weren't actually landing in customers'  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1989200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hands. Because the business recognized inventory \norders as revenue and made no distinction between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1994240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "retail and wholesale, he argued the company had \nno idea and no reason to care where shipments  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1999200,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ended up, since everyone along that leg of \nthe pyramid got paid. This was the literal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2003680,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "definition of a pyramid scheme.\nHerbalife responded by building a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2007200,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "direct-to-consumer website for every seller, \nwhere customers could buy online and have it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2011520,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shipped straight to their homes. Since the seller \nnever paid upfront or touched these orders, the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2016160,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inventory-loading accusation fell apart—how can \nyou load inventory you never hold? The websites  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2021760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also undercut Ackman's claim that no rational \ncustomer would pay full retail—yet here were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2026640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "customers doing exactly that online, with full \nfreedom to price-shop. What Ackman fundamentally  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2031760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "underestimated was the cult-like core of MLMs.\nFor all the millions Pershing Square spent on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2037280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exposés, no mass exodus ever occurred. No \nspreadsheet can capture the psychology that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2042640,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holds a pyramid together. The social bonds \nbetween sellers and customers, the daily  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2047200,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "routines the products built, and the belonging \nthe clubs provided were all far more resilient  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2051680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than Ackman had imagined. His message may have \nbeen right, but he was also the wrong messenger.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2056720,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He came across as an out-of-touch billionaire \noutsider attacking tight-knit, working-class  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2061040,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "communities who were just trying to make better \nlives for themselves. From this lens, Herbalife's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2065760,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "customers weren't really paying for nutrition. \nThey were paying for friendship and community, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2070080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the daily shot was just the price of admission. \nWith empirical proof of customer demand, the U.S.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2075600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "government had no grounds to shut Herbalife down. \nIt couldn't be branded a pyramid scheme, even if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2080800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the underlying \"why\" behind the demand was flawed.\nBut Herbalife was still charged with deception. It  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2086480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had marketed selling as a pathway to life-changing \nfinancial freedom and fortunes to millions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2091600,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when in reality those at the middle and bottom \nof the pyramid earned, on average, less than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2096320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$5 a month. It had also pushed sellers to start \nclubs as a way to supercharge their earnings,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2101600,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even though internally the company knew \nthat the average owner barely broke-even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2105600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on what they put in. The clubs were never the \nget-rich-quick path that Herbalife had sold. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2110720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The FTC fined the company $200 million and \nforced it into a leaner, tighter structure:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2116320,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at least 80% of U.S. sales now had to be \nverified retail purchases, and only one-third  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2121440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of commissions could be earned off inventory \norders. Herbalife was also forced to split its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2126480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pyramid and permanently remove the vast majority \nof \"members\" who were really just discount buyers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2131760,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "purchasing for themselves at wholesale prices. \nSellers now had to complete a full year with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2136800,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the company before opening a club, and all \nlifestyle advertising was banned. But because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2141440,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the settlement let Herbalife keep operating, \nthe stock surged to new highs and Ackman had  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2146320,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no choice but to close his position, having \nlost over a billion dollars across four years. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2151280,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Herbalife had survived Ackman. \nBut as Tupperware proved,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2156560,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no MLM can outrun its own math forever. Its core \nNorth American markets had matured, and the battle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2160800,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with Ackman had left a black cloud hanging over \nthe brand. In its strongholds of Mexico and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2166080,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "U.S., the clubs had hit saturation. Operating \ndomestically had become painful under the new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2171360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mandates from the FTC settlement. Like Tupperware \nbefore it, the company predictably turned its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2176480,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attention and investment towards developing \ncountries with weaker regulation and the massive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2180960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "underemployed, uneducated populations that MLMs \nthrive in. It ran the same playbook—tailoring  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2186960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "products to local tastes and introducing smaller, \ncheaper packaging to lower the barrier to entry so  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2192640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even the poorest could make it into the pyramid.\nBut while Herbalife still earns billions today,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2197680,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the stock has cratered 80% over the past decade. \nThe gig economy gutted its recruiting pool. Anyone  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2203760,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wanting flexible side income now opens Uber, \nDoorDash, or a local equivalent for simpler work  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2208880,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and immediate payouts, and, like Herbalife before, \nthese apps conveniently don't check immigration  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2214560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "status. Meanwhile, GLP-1 drugs have upended \nthe entire weight-loss category as objectively  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2220160,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "superior alternatives. And inflation has squeezed \nthe very clubs that kept the brand alive: a daily  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2225760,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$9 shake-and-tea combo has gone from an affordable \npastime to a luxury splurge for the working-class  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2231360,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "customers it depends on. Herbalife these days \nis stagnating rather than growing as the company  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2236320,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has simply hit the ceiling of its own pyramid.\nWhile Herbalife built its business on weight-loss  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2241200,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consumables and Tupperware on kitchen durables, \nNu Skin engineered its empire around beauty,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2247200,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scarcity, and the razor-and-razorblade model. \nIt sold proprietary $300 devices whose gels,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2253680,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cartridges, and primers had to be replaced every \n30 days—so once a seller closed the deal on the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2258960,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hardware, the customer had to keep coming back \nto them for the refills that kept it running.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2263360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In short, the device was the hook and the refills \nwere the subscription. Nu Skin pulled more money  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2268240,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out of customers than Herbalife and Tupperware, \nand built itself a predictable revenue floor in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2272960,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the process. The pitch behind it all was that Nu \nSkin's devices uniquely attacked the biological  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2278080,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sources of aging, rather than just covering up \nwrinkles. Like Herbalife, it manufactured its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2283280,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "own credibility—keeping scientists on payroll to \ndeliver presentations at global conferences and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2288480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "turn cherry-picked findings into sales ammunition. \nThe underlying message was always the same:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2293760,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these products were so advanced, so \ncutting-edge, that they could never  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2297280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be sold in an ordinary department store.\nWhile every MLM suffers from erratic revenue  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2302400,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "due to the unpredictable nature of a volunteer \nsales force, Nu Skin solved that volatility by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2307680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "institutionalizing product cycles. It fed \nprototypes and clinical data to sellers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2312480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who then drip-fed that information to customers \nto build hype months before a product ever hit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2317600,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the market. Every product launch would occur at \na big splashy convention in limited quantities.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2322880,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That artificial scarcity drove buyers to \nrush in and secure the first batch. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2327440,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "company would then pull the product off the \nmarket for months so sellers could rebuild  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2331360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the hype all over again before the wider rollout.\nNu Skin's value prop landed the hardest in East  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2337040,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Asia, where youth preservation is a cultural \nstandard and skincare is a form of social and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2341760,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "professional currency. Nearly 90% of the company's \nrevenue came from outside the United States,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2346960,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with China, Japan, and South Korea as the backbone \nof the business. It targeted middle-class women  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2352720,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who already spent heavily on premium beauty \nand wanted a high-status side hustle that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2357120,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could signal that they were on their way up.\nBut the product moat faded through the 2010s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2361440,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and 2020s. The beauty-tech market that Nu Skin \nhad helped popularize became saturated with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2366720,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cheaper competitors who didn't gate their products \nbehind MLMs, conventions, and artificial scarcity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2373280,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like the others, Nu Skin's products had to be \naggressively marked up to compensate everyone  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2377520,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the pyramid—and customers eventually realized \nthey were paying a premium for the sales channel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2382400,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather than for a better product. At the same \ntime, the overall beauty category itself evolved,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2387280,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as consumers shifted away from $100 mechanical \ndevices and toward more convenient, cheaper,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2392960,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "portable alternatives like serums and topicals.\nNu Skin has since gone down-market, away from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2398640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "high-ticket device systems and toward small, \naffordable, $20 OEM products like toothpaste,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2404320,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "face masks, and lip gloss that its sellers can \neasily pitch on social media for virtual sales.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2409760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the commission on a $20 tube of toothpaste \nis worlds away from a $300 device system,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2415200,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the low-cost line couldn't offset \nthe collapse of the premium segment. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2419280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The new generation of beauty sellers also possess \nnone of the blind loyalty of the old guard.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2424400,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Traditional sellers had local, in-person \nnetworks and had sunk thousands into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2428480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inventory and training, all of which locked them \ninto the Nu Skin pyramid. Today's social-media  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2433760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sellers have no switching costs and no interest \nin maintaining pyramids. Whenever the algorithm  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2438560,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "changes or a hotter brand emerges, they simply \nredirect their posts and their audience goes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2442960,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "along with them instead of Nu Skin.\nUltimately, the biggest disruption of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2446640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all came in Nu Skin's historic strongholds \nas Chinese, Korean, and Japanese women have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2451760,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "become the most sophisticated, price-transparent \nbeauty buyers on the planet. Broadly speaking,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2457120,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "MLMs have become obsolete. Modern consumer \nbrands today openly compete on availability,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2462000,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transparency, product, convenience, and lifetime \nvalue rather than the information arbitrage,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2466640,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "psychology, and gig workforce that \nsustain these pyramids in the first place. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2471440,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These companies all run on the same machine. \nOnce they switch on that compensation flywheel,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2475360,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it can never be switched off. To keep it spinning, \nthe company has no choice but to keep feeding new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2480320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "products, fresh incentives, and bigger dreams \nto recruits and sellers. In the short-term,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2486000,
    "dDurationMs": 873,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "MLM fuels their growth but in the long-term, \nalways turns into a cage. It structurally  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2486873,
    "dDurationMs": 567,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forces the company to keep its foot on the gas \nand ignore everything else, even if the market,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2487440,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consumer, and competition are leaving them in the \ndust. It can’t be switched off either because then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2491940,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the company would collapse. Thus, every MLM ends \nup trapped by its sellers as much as its sellers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2496080,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are trapped to them as two sides that are just \nmilking each other for everything they can get,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2500560,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with no loyalty between them and \nno vision beyond the next quarter."
    } ]
  } ]
}
